          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In a child with rickets, which pattern of lower-limb deformity corresponds to the child's age?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bow legs (genu varum) in weight-bearing infants; knock-knees (genu valgum) in older children</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Softened bone yields to gravity and muscle pull — infants who begin walking develop genu varum, while older children more often develop genu valgum.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Knock-knees in infants; bow legs in older children</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This reverses the typical pattern. Infants tend to develop bow legs, while knock-knees are characteristic of older children with rickets.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bow legs at all ages, regardless of when the child begins walking</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The deformity type changes with age and weight-bearing pattern. Bow legs predominate in early walkers, but older children are more prone to knock-knees.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Knock-knees at all ages, because rickets affects the medial growth plate preferentially</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Rickets does not selectively target the medial growth plate. The deformity pattern depends on the child's age and the mechanical forces acting on softened bone.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: weight-bearing infants with rickets develop bow legs (genu varum), while older children more often develop knock-knees (genu valgum). The difference relates to changing mechanical forces on softened bone."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. Softened rachitic bone deforms under mechanical load — the direction depends on the child's age and weight-bearing pattern, and worsens if the metabolic disease is left untreated."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
