          "label": "<p>Which metabolic bone disease characteristically produces a markedly elevated ser</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which metabolic bone disease characteristically produces a markedly elevated serum alkaline phosphatase while serum calcium, phosphate, and vitamin D remain normal?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Osteoporosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In osteoporosis all biochemical parameters — including ALP — are typically normal. The problem is bone density, not mineral metabolism.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Osteomalacia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In osteomalacia ALP is elevated, but serum calcium and phosphate are also low and vitamin D is deficient. The whole panel shifts, not just ALP.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Nutritional rickets</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Rickets shares the same biochemical fingerprint as osteomalacia — low calcium, low phosphate, elevated ALP, low vitamin D, and elevated PTH. It is not an isolated ALP rise.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Paget's disease</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Paget's disease involves intense but focal osteoblastic activity, so ALP rises dramatically while systemic calcium and phosphate homeostasis is preserved.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Think about whether the disease is focal or systemic. In Paget's, intense bone turnover is localised, so only ALP rises. In osteomalacia and rickets, systemic vitamin D deficiency pulls calcium, phosphate, and vitamin D down alongside the ALP rise."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Paget's disease produces an isolated, often dramatic ALP elevation because the remodelling is focal rather than systemic — the key biochemical clue that separates it from osteomalacia and rickets."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
